    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=1e5bff11-ae4c-4b3c-a51f-a1212f1c4119&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201571327820_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Director of Financial Aid",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-01\n\nLocation: Olivet\n\nThe University of Olivet, a liberal arts institution with a historical commitment to inclusion, diversity, and social justice, invites applications for the position of  Director of Financial Aid . The successful candidate will provide personalized financial aid services to students and their families. The University of Olivet was founded in 1844 as an institution of higher education open to all regardless of race, gender, or ability to pay. The University’s faculty and staff continue its tradition of creating and sustaining a welcoming and inclusive educational environment for students and maintaining its commitment to social justice.  The University is one of the most diverse private liberal arts institutions in the state of Michigan and actively seeks diversity in its students, faculty, and staff. The undergraduate student body is a mix of urban and rural students from a range of socioeconomic backgrounds, many of whom are first-generation university attendees. The University’s faculty and staff are committed to providing personalized, individualized attention for students who commit themselves to seeking academic and personal success.  Consistent with this history, members of historically underrepresented groups are strongly encouraged to apply. Position   Summary Reporting to the Vice President of Enrollment Management, the Director of Financial Aid [Director] provides visionary leadership and strategic direction for the financial aid team, ensuring the delivery of a comprehensive and a student service-oriented financial aid process. This position plays a pivotal role in The University’s enrollment and retention strategies, overseeing the full spectrum of a need-based financial aid program. The Director will lead and train staff in financial aid counseling, conducting thorough needs analysis, determining eligibility, awarding aid, and ensuring compliance with state and federal regulations. registration events, veteran events all with the goal of improving and maintaining retention initiatives.  They will analyze departmental operations and make evaluations to help ensure the department operates efficiently and effectively.  This is a full-time position with occasional overtime required. This position is a “hands on” position and the individual is expected to process student information as necessary to ensure students are served in a timely manner.  Education and Experience Required : Bachelor's degree with three (3) years of experience in financial aid or other equivalent direct experience, as evaluated by the VP of Enrollment Management. Preferred : Master's degree with three (3) to five (5) years of financial aid experience.   Compensation This is a full-time, 12-month, exempt position with a competitive wage and comprehensive benefits package; including generous paid holiday and leave time. Tuition programs available.   Application Process Review of applications will begin immediately. Click this link to go to the Career Center, select this position title to review the position details. Then click on the “Apply” button to complete a profile and upload a cover letter, resume, and information for three references.  Additional Position details follow. Responsibilities Develop the vision, mission, guiding principles and strategic plan for Financial Aid that are in alignment with The University's strategic plan and mission. Work to ensure seamless delivery of services and support for students through cooperative, collaborative, and timely efforts with administrators and other University personnel. Recommend and assist with development of and/or revision to policies, and procedures in compliance with federal and state regulations as they relate to specific programs and services for students. Manage staff assigned to various programs and areas within Financial Aid.  Participate in selection, training and evaluation of department personnel. Oversee the selection and distribution of institutional and endowed scholarships. Monitor the professional development activities of department personnel. Counsel new and returning students and parents with financial aid eligibility, budgeting, and financial planning for university costs, eligibility determination and personal circumstances. Assist students and families with the completion of Free Application for Federal Student Aid (FAFSA). Provide financial aid presentations at school events and area high schools. Keep abreast of state and federal financial aid regulations. Maintain both operational and student financial aid budgets for The University. Prepare and complete enrollment and financial aid reports for all internal and external constituencies including state and federal entities.  Oversee registration including the administration of holds and registration events. Ensure information on The University's website as it pertains to Financial Aid is routinely updated. Prepare documents and software for financial aid awarding season. Oversee and manage the awarding and closing out of federal, state, and institutional aid programs. Work with financial aid staff to complete state and federal verification procedures for selected applicants. Ensure communications with students, parents, staff, faculty, and administrators are timely, effective and aligned with The University's strategic plan. Performs research, statistical analysis, and reporting as requested by the VP of Enrollment Management. Assist the admissions office with recruiting, events, orientations and training. Oversee and work directly to ensure PowerFAIDS (the system of record for student financial aid) is leveraged to the best possible level of automation for the office. Establish and maintain appropriate security access in PowerFAIDS, ELM, Jenzabar, Slate and other related systems for all Financial Aid staff. Working in partnership with the Business Office, ensure all monthly and annual close out financial aid reconciliations are performed in a timely manner and in accordance with all applicable regulations. Serve as liaison between state, federal, internal, external auditors during their performance of required financial aid audits and reviews. Accountabilities Manages the Financial Aid staff. Manages Federal Title IV and State of Michigan Financial Aid Programs. Maintains relationships with professional financial aid organizations. Maintains program of compliance for all financial aid related state, federal, or NCAA and MIAA Conference monitored programs. Provides regular reporting and analysis to The University as requested and required by the VP of Enrollment Management Manages the development and disbursement of printed information regarding student financial aid, registration and student accounts. Monitors student accounts for against registration. Ensures Veteran Benefits are applied in a timely manner. Manages the operational budget for Financial Aid. Ensures financial aid awarding for new and current students is completed timely. Advises The University Administration on the impact of changes in financial aid policies. Performs duties in a diverse, challenging and stressful environment.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=1e5bff11-ae4c-4b3c-a51f-a1212f1c4119&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201567545561_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Director of Marketing",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-20\n\nLocation: Olivet\n\nDirector of Marketing   Department: Marketing and Communications Location: The University of Olivet - Olivet, Michigan Reports to: Chief Marketing Officer Salary Range: $50,000–$70,000, commensurate with experience Position Summary   The University of Olivet seeks a strategically minded, brand-aware, digitally fluent professional to serve as Director of Marketing. Working closely with the Chief Marketing Officer, the Director will help define, develop, and strengthen University of Olivet’s brand. This includes clarifying the University’s market role, communicating its distinctive value and ensuring the brand is expressed consistently across enrollment marketing, digital media, content, and institutional communications. The Director will use research, analytics, digital strategy, and artificial intelligence to increase awareness, strengthen engagement, and support enrollment growth. This is a high impact opportunity for an emerging leader to shape how prospective students, families, and the wider community perceive The University of Olivet. Responsibilities   • Work with the CMO to develop marketing strategies aligned with enrollment, revenue, and institutional aims. • Identify priority audiences, markets, programs, and growth opportunities. • Strengthen The University of Olivet’s brand positioning by leveraging value propositions and competitive differentiation. • Partner with Admissions to improve inquiry, application, deposit, and enrollment results. • Recommend how to allocate marketing resources across audiences, campaigns, and channels. • Establish performance measures that connect marketing activity to enrollment and revenue. • Use research, analytics, and testing to evaluate strategies and recommend changes. • Lead the responsible use of AI for research, segmentation, personalization, content development, forecasting, and performance analysis. • Coordinate the work of employees, agencies, freelancers, and external partners. Qualifications Required  • Bachelor’s degree in marketing, communications, business, analytics, digital media, or a comparable field. • Demonstrated strategic thinking and ability to translate company goals into executable plans. • Strong knowledge of digital marketing, audience strategy, and performance measurement. • Working knowledge of artificial intelligence and its responsible use in marketing. • Skill to interpret data and communicate its strategic implications. • Strong written, verbal, and presentation skills. • Relevant professional experience in marketing, communications, enrollment marketing, agency work, or a comparable field. • Commitment to the mission and values of the University of Olivet. Preferred • Master’s degree in a related field. • Approximately two to five years of progressive marketing experience. • Experience in higher education or another mission-focused organization. • Familiarity with enrollment funnels, CRM systems, marketing automation, analytics, or audience segmentation. • Experience leading projects, teams, budgets, agencies, or vendors. • Evidence of marketing work that produced measurable results. Higher-education experience is welcomed but not required. Candidates from agencies, consumer marketing, technology, nonprofit organizations, and other sectors are encouraged to apply. The University of Olivet is an equal-opportunity employer.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=1e5bff11-ae4c-4b3c-a51f-a1212f1c4119&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201565277089_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Director of Library and Learning Resources",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-13\n\nLocation: Olivet\n\nPosition Summary The Director of Library and Learning Resources is first and foremost an educator and an active member of the academic community. Reporting to the Dean for Academic Affairs, the Director takes initiative to engage students and faculty directly, teaching them to find, evaluate, and use information effectively and critically, and championing Burrage Library as a center of learning rather than only a place to house and manage collections. In this spirit, the Director leads the University of Olivet's library services, research support, and information literacy for both residential and online learners. Sustaining this outward-facing work requires a strong foundation. The Director also manages the collections, systems, budget, and daily operations that keep resources accessible, reliable, and current, increasingly through electronic and consortially shared resources. These operational responsibilities support the position's central purpose rather than define it: the aim is a library that actively teaches, reaches out, and serves, not one that simply maintains a space and a collection. Because the University is a small, student-centered institution, the Director also carries two closely related responsibilities that extend the library's educational mission into the wider student experience: supporting students and faculty in the use of the learning management system and related digital learning tools, and serving as the campus liaison for course materials and textbook affordability in partnership with the bookstore. Both functions draw on the expertise the Director already brings to library resources, and both keep the position focused on a single purpose, helping students obtain, afford, and effectively use the materials and tools they need to succeed in a relationship-based learning environment. Burrage Library is envisioned as the hub of academic life outside of the classroom, an active space for teaching, learning, research, and collaboration. The Director shares this vision, models active membership in the academic community, and works to create a vibrant, welcoming environment for residential undergraduates, online learners in graduate and undergraduate programs, and advanced scholars. Performance Outcomes The success of the Director of Library and Learning Resources will be demonstrated by the following: Measurable growth in student and faculty engagement with information literacy instruction, outreach, and research support. Students and faculty who can find, evaluate, and use information effectively and critically, supported by an active and visible library presence in the academic community. Effective support for faculty and students in the use of the learning management system and digital learning tools. Reduced cost of course materials to students through coordinated course materials, open educational resource, and course reserve initiatives with the bookstore. Library services, collections, and systems that reflect emerging trends and best practices for academic libraries and that deliver reliable access for on campus and online learners. A vibrant, welcoming library environment that serves as the hub of academic life and supports research, scholarship, and creativity. A balanced annual library budget that delivers resources and services efficiently. A trained, well supervised student workforce and a positive service culture. Primary Responsibilities Research Instruction, Outreach, and Library Services (approximately 65 percent of work time) Take initiative as an active, visible member of the academic community, reaching out to students, faculty, and departments to connect them with available information resources and to teach effective, critical use of those resources. Develop, deliver, and assess information literacy and research instruction for students and faculty, in person and online, building their ability to find, evaluate, and use information effectively and critically. Partner proactively with faculty and departments to integrate research and information literacy into courses and the curriculum. Provide responsive reference and research consultation to students, faculty, staff, and the community. Provide professional leadership and vision for Burrage Library as an active space for learning, research, teaching, and collaboration. Develop and maintain the library's collections, including academic research databases, electronic resources, and print materials, and establish policies for acquisition, subscription, and deaccession. Oversee daily operations, including circulation and interlibrary loan services, and the library systems and catalog, including the evaluation and implementation of new electronic resources and platforms. Aid in the development and monitoring of a balanced annual budget, including processing purchase orders, invoices, and annual standing orders in coordination with the Dean of the College. Identify and pursue consortia and shared service opportunities to expand access and control costs. Hire, train, supervise, and evaluate library student employees, interns, and volunteers in accordance with applicable laws and University policy. Contribute to a clear, realistic strategic plan for the library in alignment with the University's strategic plan, and participate in campus-wide initiatives and working groups. In accordance with FERPA and best practices, maintain the confidentiality of student records and use discretion at all times. Digital Learning and Learning Management System Support (approximately 20 percent of work time) Serve as a primary point of support for students in navigating the learning management system and related digital learning tools. Partner with faculty to embed research guides, information literacy content, and library resources within courses in the learning management system. Provide instruction and create guides, tutorials, and orientation materials that build students' digital and information literacy. Coordinate with information technology staff on digital learning tools, referring technical system administration and account issues to the appropriate office. Support online learners with equitable access to library and learning resources. Course Materials and Bookstore Liaison (approximately 15 percent of work time) Serve as the campus liaison for course materials in partnership with the bookstore, with a focus on lowering the cost of materials to students. Promote and support the adoption of open educational resources and library licensed materials as affordable alternatives to commercial textbooks. Coordinate course reserves and connect required course materials with library and electronic holdings where possible. Collaborate with faculty and the bookstore on the timely identification, availability, and accessibility of course materials each term. Supervision and General Responsibilities Work constructively and cooperatively with staff, faculty, and administrators, and collaborate across departments to foster a vibrant campus community. Actively pursue personal growth and professional development. Perform other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Master's degree in library science (MLS or MLIS) from an institution accredited by the American Library Association. A minimum of three years of experience in the successful administration of library services, with preference for academic or college library settings. Demonstrated leadership and supervisory experience, including budgeting and administrative responsibility. Demonstrated commitment to and skill in teaching or training students and faculty in the use of library and research resources. A proactive approach, ready to take initiative, with a demonstrated commitment to active, visible engagement with students, faculty, and staff in an academic environment. Preferred Qualifications Experience teaching or supporting learning in person and online. Familiarity with a learning management system and with embedding library content in online courses. Experience with open educational resources, course reserves, or textbook affordability initiatives. Experience with consortial or shared library services and electronic resource management. Demonstrated competence working with culturally diverse groups in leadership, teaching, scholarship, or service. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities A proactive orientation and genuine commitment to teaching and outreach as an active member of the academic community. Ability to design, deliver, and assess information literacy and research instruction for diverse learners in person and online. Knowledge of best practices and trends in academic librarianship, including electronic resources, information literacy, and library systems. Knowledge of collection development, circulation, interlibrary loan, and cataloging practices. Strong instructional, interpersonal, organizational, and communication skills, oral and written. Ability to use library systems, common productivity software, and a learning management system. Ability to manage a limited budget with attention to detail and accountability. Ability to build relationships and work collaboratively across campus departments. Flexibility, patience, and a student centered service orientation. Physical Demands and Working Environment The physical demands and work environment described here are representative of those an employee must meet to perform the essential functions of this position. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform these functions. Remain in a stationary position for extended periods and operate a computer and keyboard. Use common office equipment. Handle library materials weighing up to 25 pounds, with or without assistance. Traverse campus and communicate effectively with diverse constituencies. Tolerate a noise level that is usually mild to moderate. This position description is subject to change at any time with notice to the employee. Salary is commensurate with education and experience.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=1e5bff11-ae4c-4b3c-a51f-a1212f1c4119&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201553184370_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Accounting Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-03\n\nLocation: Olivet\n\nReporting to the Chief Financial Officer (CFO), the Accounting Manager oversees the day‑to‑day accounting operations of the Business Office and serves as the primary steward of the University’s financial records. This position ensures the integrity of the general ledger, manages cash monitoring and bank reconciliations, supports the preparation of internal and external financial reports, and maintains strong internal controls across financial operations. The Accounting Manager leads core accounting functions including cash management, month‑end close, general ledger oversight, and audit preparation. The role also supervises transactional financial operations including Accounts Payable and collaborates closely with Student Financial Services to ensure accurate posting of payments, charges, and adjustments to student accounts.   The Accounting Manager works closely with the CFO to support financial reporting to executive leadership, the Resources Committee, and the Board of Trustees, and ensures compliance with institutional policies, GAAP standards, and applicable federal and regulatory requirements.   The A/P specialist reports to this position.    ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS Maintain oversight of the University’s general ledger and ensure accuracy of all financial transactions. Manage daily cash monitoring including review of bank balances and coordination of transfers between accounts. Perform and review bank reconciliations and ensure timely resolution of reconciling items. Support the preparation of internal financial reports including monthly financial statements and supporting schedules. Oversee the month‑end and year‑end closing processes ensuring timely and accurate completion. Review and approve journal entries for accuracy and appropriate general ledger classification. Supervise Accounts Payable operations including invoice processing, payment batches, vendor records, and documentation controls. Coordinate with Student Financial Services to ensure accurate posting of payments, charges, and adjustments affecting student receivable accounts. Support reconciliation between the student information system and the general ledger. Prepare audit schedules and coordinate documentation required for annual financial and compliance audits. Maintain internal control procedures and segregation of duties across accounting operations. Monitor purchasing and payment processes to ensure compliance with institutional policies. Assist the CFO with financial analysis, special projects, and financial reporting initiatives.   ADDITIONAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Establish and maintain controls over cash and cash equivalent accounts. Ensure reconciliation of subsidiary ledgers to the general ledger. Support monitoring of departmental budgets in coordination with the Budget & Financial Analyst. Assist with the preparation of year‑end financial schedules and reporting requirements. Provide support for donor‑restricted fund accounting and other institutional financial reporting requirements. Maintain organized documentation to support financial reporting, audit readiness, and compliance. Collaborate with Business Office leadership to improve financial processes and operational efficiency.   KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, ABILITIES   Thorough knowledge of Generally Accepted Accounting Principles (GAAP). Understanding of financial reporting requirements applicable to higher education institutions. Strong analytical, problem‑solving, and financial reconciliation skills. Proficiency with accounting systems, spreadsheets, and financial reporting tools. Ability to review financial transactions and detect inconsistencies or errors. Strong written and verbal communication skills. Ability to work collaboratively across departments including Student Financial Services, Human Resources, and academic units. High level of integrity, professional ethics, and attention to detail. Ability to manage multiple priorities and meet reporting deadlines.   EDUCATION AND EXPERIENCE   Bachelor’s degree in Accounting required. Master’s degree in Accounting, Finance, or Business Administration preferred. CPA or CPA‑track strongly preferred. Minimum of 6–8 years of progressive accounting experience, preferably within higher education, nonprofit, or similarly complex organizational environments. Experience with financial reporting, bank reconciliations, general ledger management, and audit preparation is required.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=1e5bff11-ae4c-4b3c-a51f-a1212f1c4119&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201557111215_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Graduate Assistantship for Residential Life",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-21\n\nLocation: Olivet\n\nPOSITION SUMMARY The Graduate Assistant for Residence Life is a live-in professional staff member who supports residence hall operations, student development, community building, student success initiatives, and departmental administration. As a member of the Residence Life leadership team, the Graduate Assistant assists with supervision, programming, crisis response, student conduct, housing operations, and residence hall management. This position provides practical experience in higher education, student affairs, leadership development, and residence life administration while pursuing graduate studies. The Graduate Assistant is expected to support the mission, vision, and values of The University of Olivet. ESSENTIAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Residence Hall Administration Assist with the operation of assigned residence halls housing approximately 100–300 students. Maintain visibility and accessibility within the residential community. Conduct facility inspections and identify maintenance, safety, and housekeeping concerns. Submit and monitor work requests in coordination with Facilities Management. Assist with room assignments, room changes, occupancy management, and housing records. Support opening, closing, break housing, transition processes, occupancy verification, and room condition assessments. Student Development and Community Building Develop and implement educational, social, wellness, diversity, and community-building programs. Support student engagement initiatives that promote retention and academic success. Foster an inclusive residential environment and encourage student leadership. Assist with campus-wide programming and engagement efforts. Resident Assistant Leadership and Support Assist with the recruitment, selection, training, and development of Resident Assistants. Participate in pre-service and ongoing staff training. Serve as a mentor and resource for Resident Assistants. Support staff development, community management, and student support efforts. Student Conduct and Conflict Resolution Assist with roommate mediation and conflict resolution. Address policy violations according to University procedures. Document incidents accurately and conduct assigned preliminary conduct meetings. Educate students on community standards and support accountability efforts. Crisis Response and On-Call Responsibilities Participate in the professional staff on-call rotation. Respond to emergencies, behavioral incidents, facility concerns, and crisis situations. Provide support during medical emergencies, mental health concerns, welfare checks, missing student incidents, and other campus emergencies. Complete incident reports and collaborate with Campus Safety, Counseling Services, Facilities, and other campus partners. Administrative Responsibilities Attend staff and supervision meetings. Assist with departmental projects, assessment initiatives, communications, and housing correspondence. Maintain accurate records and documentation. Provide customer service and office support as needed. Assist with summer housing operations when applicable. Student Success and Retention Support student outreach and retention initiatives. Assist students experiencing academic, social, personal, or transitional challenges. Refer students to appropriate campus resources. UNIVERSITY SERVICE Demonstrate professionalism and commitment to the mission and values of The University of Olivet. Serve on departmental and university committees as assigned. Participate in major campus events and student engagement initiatives. Perform additional duties as assigned by the Assistant Dean of Student Life. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Required Admission to and active enrollment in a graduate degree program. Bachelor's degree from an accredited institution. Strong organizational, interpersonal, written, and verbal communication skills. Ability to work independently and collaboratively in a fast-paced environment. Ability to maintain confidentiality and exercise sound judgment. Availability for evening, weekend, and break-period responsibilities. Valid driver's license with an acceptable driving record. Preferred Experience in residence life, student affairs, orientation, leadership, or student services. Experience with conflict mediation and student development. Experience supervising or mentoring student leaders. Knowledge of housing management software and student conduct processes. REQUIRED COMPETENCIES Leadership and teamwork Student development theory Crisis management Conflict resolution and mediation Event planning and program development Administrative organization Customer service Diversity, equity, inclusion, and belonging practices Professional communication Risk management WORK SCHEDULE This position requires 20–25 hours per week, On call rotations, and may include: Daytime office hours Evening duty responsibilities Weekend coverage Opening and closing periods Training sessions University events The Graduate Assistant must reside in assigned campus housing and remain available to fulfill live-in responsibilities throughout the appointment period. LEARNING OUTCOMES Upon completion of the assistantship, the Graduate Assistant will have gained experience in: Residence hall administration and housing operations Student conduct administration Crisis response and risk management Student leadership development Event planning and assessment Student retention strategies Supervision and staff development Higher education administration Student affairs practice EVALUATION Performance evaluations will occur at least once per semester and assess: Job performance Professionalism Initiative and leadership Student interactions Administrative effectiveness Crisis response Program development and implementation Contribution to departmental goals The University of Olivet is committed to fostering student success, personal growth, leadership development, and community engagement. Graduate Assistants are expected to advance these values through their work within the residential community. Compensation and Benefits $500/month stipend Campus apartment provided. University meal plan included during Academic Year. Utilities, internet, and on-site laundry included at no cost. Access to campus fitness facilities. Campus parking provided. Valuable professional experience in residence life and student affairs administration.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=1e5bff11-ae4c-4b3c-a51f-a1212f1c4119&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201552762565_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructors for Fall 2026",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-08\n\nLocation: Olivet\n\nAdjunct Instructors for Fall 2026 Criminal Justice (Corrections), Economics, Marketing and Sales, Financial Planning, Personal Training, Communications, Mathematics, Psychology The University of Olivet, a liberal arts institution with an historical commitment to inclusion, diversity, and social justice, seeks in-person  Adjunct Instructors  for our general pool and for specific subjects for on-campus classes during the Fall 2026 semester. This posting is for in-person, daytime or evening instruction on The University of Olivet campus. Adjunct instructor positions are part-time, and teaching assignments will vary from semester to semester, with most classes meeting twice each week for 15 weeks. Students from underrepresented minorities constitute 26% of the current student body, with almost 20% of the student body identifying as African American. Approximately half of the student body is Pell grant eligible. Members of historically underrepresented groups are strongly encouraged to apply. Primary responsibilities:  Evaluate and grade students’ class work, assignments and papers. Prepare and deliver instructional materials (i.e. syllabi, homework assignments, and handouts) in-person and via the Online Learning Management System (Canvas). Initiate, facilitate, and moderate classroom discussions. Compile, administer, and grade assessments. Participate in required reporting processes (i.e. grades, attendance, etc.). Qualifications : Master’s degree in specific subject or a related field; or, a Master’s degree in a related field with at least 18 graduate credits the specific field. We seek candidates who are open to innovation and collaboration, who embrace Olivet’s relationship-based approach to pedagogy, and who embody our commitment to inclusivity. Starting Date: Review of applications will begin immediately and continue as needed. Anticipated start date is August 12, 2026 for optional trainings. Fall semester courses run August 24- December 11, 2026. Application Process:  Click on this link to go to the online application site:  Career Center Once in the Career Center, click on the job title to view the posting and click “Apply” to set up a profile and upload the following materials: Letter of application; Current resume or curriculum vitae with contact information for three references; One-page statement of your interest in and experience with working with a diverse population of students in a multi-ethnic environment; Transcript from the highest degree-granting institution related to the position (official or unofficial) and/or documentation of graduate coursework in the area of instruction;  Sample syllabus (optional); and Student evaluations (optional). Offer of hire is contingent upon receipt of official transcripts, completion of a background check, and verification of information submitted. * * * * * The University of Olivet was founded in 1844 as an institution of higher education open to all regardless of race, gender, or ability to pay. The university’s faculty and staff continue its tradition of creating and sustaining a welcoming and inclusive educational environment for students and maintain its commitment to social justice. The university is one of the most diverse private liberal arts institutions in the state of Michigan and actively seeks diversity in its students, faculty, and staff. The undergraduate student body is a mix of urban and rural students from a range of socioeconomic backgrounds, many of whom are first-generation college attendees. The university’s faculty and staff are committed to providing personalized, individualized attention for students who commit themselves to seeking academic and personal success. Located in Olivet, Michigan, the university is within driving distance of Lansing, Battle Creek, and Kalamazoo; two hours from the metropolitan Detroit area; and three hours from Chicago.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=1e5bff11-ae4c-4b3c-a51f-a1212f1c4119&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201547391425_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Adjunct Nursing",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-06-22\n\nLocation: Olivet\n\nClinical or Lab Rotations The University of Olivet, a liberal arts institution with a historical commitment to inclusion, diversity, and social justice, seeks in-person Adjunct Instructors in Nursing for the Fall 2026 semester and beyond. In particular, instructors with experience in clinical, Skills Lab or Sim Lab are encouraged to apply. This posting represents positions available for in-person, usually 0630-1900 on Tuesdays at various facilities, with labs or Sim labs on the Olivet campus. Adjunct instructor positions are part-time. Teaching assignments will vary from semester to semester, with most classes meeting once each week for 12 weeks. The University of Olivet's Nursing program is a clinically integrated model that emphasizes hands-on skill development, evidence-based practice, and professional formation while welcoming students at varying levels of clinical experience. We currently offer instruction across clinical practice settings, nursing simulation labs, and skills laboratory environments in small, relationship-focused cohorts. Alongside technical clinical competency, the program emphasizes a broad understanding of health across the lifespan and diverse patient populations. The program intentionally engages questions of ethical responsibility, professional character, and purposeful patient-centered care, culminating in experiences that integrate nursing practice with Olivet's commitment to Education for Individual and Social Responsibility. Students from underrepresented minorities constitute 26% of the current student body, with almost 20% of the student body identifying as African American. Approximately half of the student body is Pell grant eligible. Members of historically underrepresented groups are strongly encouraged to apply. Primary Responsibilities: Evaluate and assess student performance in clinical locations, skills laboratory, and/or simulation settings using established competency checklists and clinical evaluation tools. Prepare and deliver instructional materials (i.e., lab guides, simulation scenarios, skills demonstration instructions, and clinical objectives) in-person and via the Online Learning Management System. Facilitate, guide, and debrief students during clinical experiences, skills practice sessions, and/or simulation scenarios to promote critical thinking and clinical reasoning. Compile, administer, and grade clinical performance assessments, skills checkoffs, and simulation debriefs. Participate in required reporting processes (i.e., clinical attendance, competency completion, academic concern reports, and grade submission). Qualifications: Earned Bachelor of Science in Nursing (BSN) required. We seek candidates who are open to innovation and collaboration, who embrace Olivet’s relationship-based approach to pedagogy, and who embody our commitment to inclusivity. Starting Date:  Review of applications will begin immediately and continue as needed. Anticipated start date is August 2026. Application Process: Click on this link to go to the online application site: Click Here   Once in the Career Center, click on the job title to view the posting and click “Apply” to set up a profile and upload the following materials: 1. Letter of application; 2. Current resume or curriculum vitae with contact information for three references; 3. One-page statement of your interest in and experience with working with a diverse population of students in a multi-ethnic environment; 4. Transcript from the highest degree-granting institution related to the position (official or unofficial) and/or documentation of graduate coursework in the area of instruction; 5. Sample syllabus for a college writing class; and 6. Student evaluations (optional). Offer of hire is contingent upon receipt of official transcripts, completion of a background check, and verification of information submitted. * * * * * The University of Olivet was founded in 1844 as an institution of higher education open to all regardless of race, gender, or ability to pay. The university’s faculty and staff continue its tradition of creating and sustaining a welcoming and inclusive educational environment for students and maintain its commitment to social justice. The university is one of the most diverse private liberal arts institutions in the state of Michigan and actively seeks diversity in its students, faculty, and staff. The undergraduate student body is a mix of urban and rural students from a range of socioeconomic backgrounds, many of whom are first-generation college attendees. The university’s faculty and staff are committed to providing personalized, individualized attention for students who commit themselves to seeking academic and personal success. Located in Olivet, Michigan, the university is within driving distance of Lansing, Battle Creek, and Kalamazoo; two hours from the metropolitan Detroit area; and three hours from Chicago.",
